;;; A pattern inside a pattern whose relation no parameter binds matches
;;; nothing, in silence — M1e2 **W24**, register row
;;; `W24/nested-unbound-head` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), banked by
;;; S1e2.2.24 (2026-11-05) from the inventory of 2026-11-05's W-14 (its probe
;;; `nested_unbound_head.ein`, the program unchanged), ruled by the user on
;;; 2026-11-05 (Q-M1e2.16 (1)).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact, `(box (a b))`, and one rule whose pattern `(box (?r b))` names
;;; the relation of its inner pattern by `?r`, a variable the rule has no
;;; parameter for.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A relation is named by a name or by a parameter its activator binds
;;; (Q29), and `?r` is neither. The user ruled that such a pattern is
;;; refused at load, as a range and a head `_` are, with a line in Ein's
;;; spelling naming the rule and the clause. Every command that loads the
;;; program exits 1 (`ein test`: 2). The same pattern at the top level,
;;; `(?r b)`, stopped compilation until then
;;; (`examples/ein-bugs/unbound-relation-head.ein`), and is refused at load
;;; with the same line since.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and why it does not any more ─────────────
;;;
;;; Until S1e2.2.24 the program loaded, and the rule compiler left `(?r b)`
;;; an opaque slot (`compile.rs`' `slot`, `plan::Slot::Opaque`), which
;;; unifies with nothing, so `r` never fired: root held no `hit`, exit 0,
;;; with no word of `?r` (`ein saturate --dump FILE` and `ein solve FILE` at
;;; `32736ac8`, release and debug; every mode and `ein oracle` the same). Since S1e2.2.24
;;; the loader refuses it: *kb load error: rule 'r': its :match writes (?r
;;; b) with ?r as its relation, which no parameter binds — a relation is
;;; named by a name or by a parameter its activator binds*; the top-level
;;; `(?r b)` gets the same line.

(relation box Node :why "{?1} is in a box") (relation hit Node :why "{?1} is hit") (relation a Node :why "a holds of {?1}")
(box (a b))
(rule r () :match (box (?r b)) :assert (hit z) :why "some fact about b is in a box, so z is hit")
